      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the quest for the perfect deck build in the land of Vancouver, using the sturdy Western Red Cedar. Now, when it comes to sizing calculations, you're looking at two main types of loads: live loads and dead loads. Imagine live loads as the mischievous goblins that come and go, causing a bit of a ruckus. These are the people, furniture, and anything else that moves around on your deck. Dead loads, on the other hand, are like the steadfast, ever-present goblins that live in the forest, always there, always contributing to the weight of your deck. These are the materials themselves, like the Western Red Cedar boards, nails, and screws. Understanding the difference between these two types of loads is crucial for ensuring your post spacing is just right, so your deck can withstand the occasional goblin party without collapsing.",
