      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion earlier. Let's dive straight into the technical details for your deck build in Vancouver, focusing on the weight of the wood versus people, and the standard psf (pounds per square foot) values.\n\nFor Western Red Cedar, the dead load (the weight of the wood and other permanent fixtures) typically ranges from 3 to 5 psf. This includes the weight of the cedar boards, nails, screws, and any other permanent components of your deck. The exact value can vary based on the thickness and type of cedar used, as well as the density of the wood.\n\nLive load, which refers to the weight of people, furniture, and other temporary objects on the deck, is generally estimated at 40 psf. This value is a standard assumption used in deck design and can vary depending on the expected use of the deck. For example, a deck used for a family gathering might have a higher live load than one used for occasional picnics.\n\nWhen calculating the total load on your deck, you add the dead load and the live load together. For example, if your deck has a dead load of 4 psf and you expect a live load of 40 psf, the total load would be",
